 front-wheel drive car makes  the dash from zero to 108 seconds flat  hyundai claims an average fuel  consumption of 7.1 liters per 100  kilometers co2 emissions rating is 165  grams per kilometer  likes all the included extras hyundai  and most other asian manufacturers  provide the veloster has steering wheel  heating a gps navigation system and a  keyless ghost system with start stop  button  of course no car with a heated steering  wheel lacks heated seats  and it's all markedly lower priced than  the german and european competition so  what's not to like asmatas  the materials in the interior aren't  luxury leather or wood paneling but  montes thinks the interior looks good  enough along with being functional and  easy to keep clean  his alter ego in the back seat feels  cooped up by the low roof and wants out  and that's not so easy either  which brings us back to the point at the  beginning  why does the veloster have two doors on  the right  the developers wanted to build a car  that passengers could get out of more  safely and easily but matas reckons it's  at least as awkward to leave the back  seat of the three-door veloster as to  get out of a two-door vehicle  here australia  you
 

